        <p>I have a small number of Grok Bot invites to give away with this kit. Seats are limited, because each referral code can only be redeemed a limited number of times.</p>
        <p>What you get, officially:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A 2-week free trial of Premium.</li>
          <li>A credit card is required to start the trial.</li>
          <li>Nothing is charged at checkout.</li>
          <li>You get a reminder before the first charge, so you can cancel if you don't want to keep it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Here is the invite: <a href="https://grok.com/refer/bot?code=NNCG9QYS8SDS">https://grok.com/refer/bot?code=NNCG9QYS8SDS</a></p>
        <p>This is a Grok Bot trial invite, not a newsletter referral. You don't need Grok Bot to use the rest of this kit.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>Now, let's jump in.</p>
      </div>

      <nav class="contents" aria-label="Contents">
        <p class="result-section-label">Contents</p>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#build-the-perfect-upwork-profile">Build the perfect Upwork profile</a></li>
          <li><a href="#upwork-job-search-tips">Upwork job search tips</a></li>
          <li><a href="#apply-for-jobs-research-before-you-write-an-upwork-proposal">Apply for jobs: research before you write an Upwork proposal</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-conversational-cover-letter-upwork-proposal-template">The conversational cover letter (Upwork proposal template)</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-to-reply-to-clients-on-upwork">How to reply to clients on Upwork</a></li>
          <li><a href="#conclusion">Conclusion</a></li>
          <li><a href="#bonus-material-and-extra-reading">Bonus material and extra reading</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <section class="kit-section" id="build-the-perfect-upwork-profile">
        <h2>Build the perfect Upwork profile</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Before you start submitting proposals and sending out cover letters, you need to make sure your profile is ready.</p>
          <p>Your profile is very important for building trust and getting clients to want to work with you. And if your proposal does its job, they will be checking your profile.</p>
          <p>Here are a few tips on making sure your profile helps convince potential clients that they should invite you to an interview.</p>
          <p>Let's start with your headline.</p>
          <h3>Headline</h3>
          <p>Let's make it very clear what you can do and who you can help.</p>
          <p>Your headline should communicate how you can help and be compelling enough to get them to continue reading your bio overview.</p>
          <p>Try to include: Job/Service Keyword + Industry + Your Niche (Specialty) If Possible</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Honest Google Ads, Facebook Ads and Music Marketing Help (example: before I niched down)</li>
            <li>The Last Google Ads Person You'll Ever Have To Hire [Free Consult]</li>
            <li>Honest Google Ads PPC Lead Generation and Strategy For National Brands</li>
            <li>Honest Google Ads PPC Lead Gen &amp; Strategy For 1 To 30 Employee Brands</li>
            <li>Honest Google Ads Management &amp; Lead Generation For Small Sales Teams (current)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="kit-figure">
          <img
            src="/images/guides/upwork-profile-mobile.jpg"
            alt="Corey Koehler's main Upwork profile on a phone, with the headline Honest Google Ads Management and Lead Generation For Small Sales Teams and a $93 hourly rate."
            width="885"
            height="702"
            loading="lazy"
            onerror="this.parentElement.style.display='none'"
          />
          <figcaption>My main Upwork profile: headline plus hourly rate. Screenshots may be slightly out of date.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <aside class="note">
          <p class="note-label">Note</p>
          <p>"[Free Consult]" in a headline was an old example.</p>
        </aside>

        <div class="prose">
          <h3>Specialized profiles</h3>
          <p>Upwork now lets you add specialized profiles on top of your main one. Each one gets its own headline, rate, overview, and work history for one service.</p>
          <p>Mine for search engine marketing reads: Honest Google Search Engine Marketing B2B &amp; B2C Manufacturing Brands. Same formula. Service keyword, industry, niche.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="kit-figure">
          <img
            src="/images/guides/upwork-profile-specialized.jpg"
            alt="Corey Koehler's specialized Upwork profile for search engine marketing. The headline is Honest Google Search Engine Marketing B2B and B2C Manufacturing Brands, with its own rate and stats."
            width="1203"
            height="715"
            loading="lazy"
            onerror="this.parentElement.style.display='none'"
          />
          <figcaption>A specialized profile. One service, one niche, its own rate and stats. Screenshots may be slightly out of date.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="prose">
          <p>Next, let's make sure that your overview (or Bio) is ready.</p>
          <h3>Overview</h3>
          <p>First thing you need to understand, and the first thing people miss, is that your overview is NOT about you.</p>
          <p>Your overview is all about your potential client and how you are going to help them solve their problem.</p>
          <p>Start by identifying their problem and making an offer with a strong call to action. This will help you stand out.</p>
          <p>See how my specialized profile opens with two questions about the client, not a paragraph about me.</p>
          <p>To get a better understanding, watch the video below from a guy named Lex DeVille.</p>
          <p>Lex doesn't look like it, but he is an Upwork guru. He's an excellent sales copywriter and has done very well on Upwork. In fact, he even deleted his profile and started one from scratch just to show newbies that they don't need experience when they use the right approach.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="note">
          <p class="note-label">Note</p>
          <p>Don't copy the delete-and-restart part. A second account, or a reset used as a loophole, can break Upwork's account rules.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Watch this video with 5 Bio Tips (you can skip to about 3:00 to get to the info):</p>
        </div>
        <div class="video-block">
          <h3 class="video-block-title">5 Upwork Bio Tips for Beginners in 2021</h3>
          <div class="video-embed">
            <button
              type="button"
              class="video-facade"
              data-youtube="AxPnHGxdhdw"
              data-title="5 Upwork Bio Tips for Beginners in 2021"
            >
              <img
                src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/AxPnHGxdhdw/hqdefault.jpg"
                alt=""
                width="480"
                height="360"
                loading="lazy"
              />
              <span class="video-play" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22"><path d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>
              </span>
              <span class="sr-only">Play video: 5 Upwork Bio Tips for Beginners in 2021</span>
            </button>
            <noscript>
              <iframe
                src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/AxPnHGxdhdw"
                title="5 Upwork Bio Tips for Beginners in 2021"
                loading="lazy"
                allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
                allowfullscreen
              ></iframe>
            </noscript>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AxPnHGxdhdw">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AxPnHGxdhdw</a></p>
          <h3>More examples</h3>
          <p>Not saying mine is perfect or the best example, BUT you can look at it to see my latest effort:</p>
          <p><a href="https://www.upwork.com/freelancers/~019f6a8ad3edfd3433">https://www.upwork.com/freelancers/~019f6a8ad3edfd3433</a></p>
          <p>And, you'll want to check out Upwork's blog post on helping your profile stand out.</p>
          <p><a href="https://www.upwork.com/resources/9-tips-create-freelancer-profile-stands-out">https://www.upwork.com/resources/9-tips-create-freelancer-profile-stands-out</a></p>
          <h3>How I'd do this with AI today: profile</h3>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>Paste this into Grok Bot or any AI agent. Then paste your current profile and a short list of your skills. Edit everything it gives you. It's your name on the profile.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <div class="ap-head">
            <p class="prompt-label">Profile rewrite prompt</p>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>You are helping me rewrite my Upwork profile so it is about the client, not about me.

My skills and background: [paste your skills, years, industries, tools]
The kind of client I want: [who they are, how big, what industry]
The problem I solve for them: [in plain words]
My current headline and overview: [paste them, or write "none yet"]
Real results or testimonials I can use: [only real ones, or "none yet"]

Please give me:
1. Five headline options using this formula: Job/Service Keyword + Industry + Niche. Keep each under 70 characters.
2. An overview that opens with one or two questions about the client's problem, then explains how I help, then ends with a clear call to action to message me on Upwork.
3. If I offer more than one service, suggest one specialized profile per service, with its own headline and a two-line opener.

Rules: plain, friendly language. No hype. No em dashes. Do not invent results, clients, or numbers. No email, phone, or outside links.</pre>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Ok, before we get to the cover letter, let's talk about the job search quick...</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="kit-section" id="upwork-job-search-tips">
        <h2>Upwork job search tips</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Getting a consistent flow of interviews and clients is a numbers game.</p>
          <p>Every day, I would look for jobs that I thought I could do and apply to at least 5 of them (more if I had the time). Results: I was getting on the phone with about a person a day on average and getting 1 to 2 projects a week. That was my experience back then, not a promise.</p>
          <p>Make searching for and applying for jobs a habit. Set aside 30 minutes to an hour every day until you have as many clients as you want.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="kit-figure">
          <img
            src="/images/guides/upwork-job-search.jpg"
            alt="An Upwork job search results page. Each listing shows the job title, hourly or fixed price, experience level, and skill tags."
            width="1208"
            height="720"
            loading="lazy"
            onerror="this.parentElement.style.display='none'"
          />
          <figcaption>An Upwork job search. Check the job title, fixed price or hourly, experience level, and the skill tags. Screenshots may be slightly out of date.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <aside class="note">
          <p class="note-label">Note</p>
          <p>Every proposal now costs Connects, and each Connect is $0.15. The job post shows how many it takes. Five a day adds up, so apply to the jobs you can really do.</p>
        </aside>
        <h3 class="block-title">How I'd do this with AI today: finding jobs</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Upwork now has an official connector (MCP) that lets an AI agent work with your Upwork account after you sign in. Here's how I'd use it:</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="step-list">
          <li>Connect your agent at <a href="https://www.upwork.com/ai/mcp">https://www.upwork.com/ai/mcp</a>. The connector is free. Normal Upwork fees and Connects still apply.</li>
          <li>Ask it to search jobs and pull matches based on your profile.</li>
          <li>Have it sort and summarize. You still pick the jobs.</li>
          <li>Let it draft. Nothing gets submitted and no Connects get spent until you approve.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Grok Bot isn't on Upwork's official setup list (Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor, and Codex are), but it's in the Grok Bot plugin directory and I got it connected for a couple of days. If sign-in doesn't work for you, search Upwork by hand and paste jobs into the prompt below. Don't try to automate the Upwork website.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="note">
          <p class="note-label">Note</p>
          <p>Don't set a scheduled search to run every few minutes. Upwork limits how many requests an agent can make. Once or twice a day is plenty. See Rate limits for Upwork MCP.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <div class="ap-head">
            <p class="prompt-label">Job search prompt</p>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>Search Upwork jobs for me. I do: [your service, in plain words]. Also search the tools and platforms around it: [list tools, e.g. QuickBooks, SolidWorks, Beehiiv].

Only show jobs posted in the last [number] days, with [hourly or fixed-price], budget at least [amount], and client payment verified.

For each match, give me one line: job title, budget, Connects needed, client hire rate, and why it fits my profile. Sort best fit first. Do not apply, draft, or spend Connects. I'll pick.</pre>
        </div>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <div class="ap-head">
            <p class="prompt-label">Screen-a-job prompt</p>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>Here is a job post: [paste the job post]
Here is my profile summary: [paste your headline and overview]

Tell me in five short lines:
1. Can I honestly do this? Yes, maybe, or no, and why.
2. What the client really wants, in their words.
3. Any red flags (vague scope, tiny budget, asks to work off Upwork).
4. Is it worth the Connects?
5. One specific detail I should mention in my cover letter.</pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="kit-section" id="apply-for-jobs-research-before-you-write-an-upwork-proposal">
        <h2>Apply for jobs: research before you write an Upwork proposal</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Now, you're ready to start applying for jobs.</p>
          <p>The following template is the key to getting potential clients to start "conversations" with you (that's where I got the name "Conversational Cover Letter").</p>
          <p>Most freelancers just copy-paste the same exact cover letter that they use for every single job. This template will make you stand out because you are telling them you can solve their problem and showing them that you are ready to help them solve their problem right away.</p>
          <p>Also, doing a little research on them will really help you customize the cover letter.</p>
          <p>You should...</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Look at their profile</li>
            <li>Look at the past jobs they've hired for</li>
            <li>See if you can find their website</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Yes, it takes extra time but it is worth it. This extra information could be the one thing that helps you stand out because you took the time to get to know them. Not just copied and pasted the same old script you use for everyone.</p>
          <p>There are 3 important things that will help your cover letter stand out.</p>
          <ol>
            <li>Invite them to a FREE 30-minute call. Many other freelancers will not get on a call until they are getting paid. This is a mistake. Many times a short conversation is all it takes for the client to realize you are the right fit, and you'll close them right on the call. If they like you, many times they aren't going to keep digging through applications from other freelancers. They want to check this off their to-do list. Bottom line, get the conversation started in the cover letter and keep it going with a call.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <aside class="note">
          <p class="note-label">Note</p>
          <p>Updated for today: before a contract starts, keep the call inside Upwork Messages. The phone icon in the thread starts a Zoom or voice call. No Calendly or personal Zoom links in a proposal.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="prose">
          <ol start="2">
            <li>Make them the charity donation guarantee. I learned this in a Perry Marshall consulting course WAY back in 2013 and it works great. It shows that you are confident in your skills and are willing to put your money where your mouth is. And don't worry, in over 10 years of doing this, I've never had a single person ask for the donation. Just in case though, use the highest dollar amount you are comfortable with.</li>
            <li>Add testimonials from past clients. Testimonials are huge. You may have some on your profile already, but add them below your signature as well in case they don't get there. This also gives you a chance to use testimonials from clients that weren't on Upwork. You could also mix and match testimonials using results to more closely match the specific job in question. Show prospective clients that you were able to deliver results.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <aside class="note">
          <p class="note-label">Note</p>
          <p>Only use testimonials you have permission to share.</p>
        </aside>
        <h3 class="block-title">Bonus script for new Upwork freelancers</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>If you are just starting out on Upwork, you are not going to have much experience on display on your profile. To build some trust, offer to do the job at a discount. Just word it in a way that doesn't impact your future earnings, like so:</p>
        </div>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <div class="ap-head">
            <p class="prompt-label">New to Upwork, only if it's true</p>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>P.S. I'm fairly new to Upwork and need to establish my credibility here so I'm willing to take this job at a 50% discount on my regular rate for the first milestone. Any future jobs, projects or monthly work will be at my regular rate when you decide to continue with me.</pre>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>That script also works great if you are trying to break into another niche but don't have much experience. For instance, let's say you are a Social Media VA but want to try getting more work as a Facebook ads manager. You could use something like this to get your foot in the door.</p>
          <h3>How I'd do this with AI today: client research</h3>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>This is the "little research" step, done faster. The agent reads. You decide what's worth mentioning.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <div class="ap-head">
            <p class="prompt-label">Client research prompt</p>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>Here is a job post and what I can see about the client:
Job post: [paste the job post]
Client info: [paste their Upwork client profile details: location, hire rate, total spent, past job titles, reviews]
Their website, if I found it: [URL or "not found"]

Give me:
1. Three specific things about this client I could mention in a cover letter.
2. What they seem to care about most, based on past hires and reviews.
3. Which of my testimonials fits best: [paste your testimonials]
4. Any questions I should ask them in the cover letter.
Keep it short. Do not make anything up. If you are not sure, say so.</pre>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Ok, finally, here is the cover letter...</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="kit-section" id="the-conversational-cover-letter-upwork-proposal-template">
        <h2>The conversational cover letter (Upwork proposal template)</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Ok, here you go. Use this template with the modifications listed above.</p>
          <p>The whole point is to start a conversation. You're not trying to close the deal in the letter. You're trying to get them talking to you.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tabs" data-tabs>
          <div class="tablist" role="tablist" aria-label="Cover letter template">
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="letter-tab-1" aria-controls="letter-panel-1" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">The letter</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="letter-tab-2" aria-controls="letter-panel-2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">New-freelancer P.S.</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="letter-tab-3" aria-controls="letter-panel-3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Testimonials</button>
          </div>
          <div role="tabpanel" id="letter-panel-1" aria-labelledby="letter-tab-1" class="is-active" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">The letter</h3>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">The conversational cover letter</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>Hello from [your state] [or use their name if you can see it].

I can help you [add task they are looking for help with using the exact words they use]

But instead of wasting your time by telling you why I am the best person for this project, I am going to show you.

[Can you tell me what your product or service is? I can give you some quick examples of how I can help you.]

Then, how about we get together for a 30-minute call right here in Upwork Messages? I think you will find that a quick chat will give you answers to lingering questions and help you make a decision. And I guarantee you will get loads of valuable insight and good value from it. If you do not, I will make a $20 donation to the charity of your choice.

Thanks,
[Your name]</pre>
            </div>
            <aside class="pro-tip">
              <p class="pro-tip-label">
                <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
                Pro tip
              </p>
              <p>Clients only see the first two lines in the results list. Make line 2, "I can help you...", do the heavy lifting.</p>
            </aside>
          </div>
          <div role="tabpanel" id="letter-panel-2" aria-labelledby="letter-tab-2" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">New-freelancer P.S.</h3>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">Only if it's true</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>P.S. I'm fairly new to Upwork and need to establish my credibility here so I'm willing to take this job at a 50% discount on my regular rate for the first milestone. Any future jobs or monthly work will be at my regular rate when you decide to continue with me.</pre>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div role="tabpanel" id="letter-panel-3" aria-labelledby="letter-tab-3" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">Testimonials</h3>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">Testimonial from current and former clients</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>"Corey gives me up to date, thorough assessments of how we're doing, no sugar coating and he hands me the results. I have seen a big spike in activity off our website and we're getting more calls from customers who could have only found us by doing a search. We're on the map now... Business is up 33%. We made the right move." Dan Miller, Operations Manager, Oz Lifting Products

"Corey has increased our businesses a lot in a short time. Looking forward to working with him more." Tyler Pope, Owner, 3D Printing Ally</pre>
            </div>
            <aside class="note">
              <p class="note-label">Note</p>
              <p>These are my testimonials, as examples. Swap in your own: "[Short quote.]" - [First name, or "a recent client"].</p>
            </aside>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Copy, paste and tweak. That's it. Then watch your messages for a reply.</p>
          <h3>How I'd do this with AI today: the conversational cover letter</h3>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>Paste this prompt into Grok Bot or any agent. Then paste the job post and your Knowledge Section. Edit before you send. I haven't written proposals with an agent yet. This is the prompt I'd start with.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <div class="ap-head">
            <p class="prompt-label">Conversational cover letter prompt</p>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>You are my Upwork proposal drafter. Your job is to draft a short Conversational Cover Letter and screening-question answers that I can edit and send. The goal of the letter is to start a conversation, not to close the deal. You do not send anything. You do not invent jobs, results, tools, or testimonials I did not give you.

Inputs I will provide:
- The job post (pasted, including budget, duration, and any screening questions)
- Optional: notes from my client research (client profile, past hires, their website)
- My Knowledge Section (below)

Use this structure:
- Line 1: "Hello [Name]," if the client's name is visible, otherwise "Hello from [my state],"
- Line 2: "I can help you [the task, using the client's exact words]."
- "But instead of wasting your time by telling you why I am the best person for this project, I am going to show you."
- One question about their product or service, plus one or two quick, specific ideas for how I'd help
- Invite a 30-minute call right here in Upwork Messages
- The $20 charity guarantee, unless I said to skip it: "I guarantee you will get loads of valuable insight and good value from it. If you do not, I will make a $20 donation to the charity of your choice."
- "Thanks," and my name only
- The new-freelancer P.S., only if I said it applies and it is true
- Then a short testimonials block, using only testimonials I provided that match this job

Hard rules:
1. Stay on Upwork. No phone, email, personal website, LinkedIn, Calendly link, or personal Zoom link.
2. About 200 to 300 words.
3. Use the client's own words. No generic copy-paste.
4. Do not promise income, rankings, or timelines I did not state.
5. Plain, friendly voice. Short paragraphs. No hype. No em dashes.
6. Answer every screening question separately, short and direct.

After the letter, give me a short note with: the exact two-line preview, which job details you used, whether to boost (only if the fit is strong), and a reminder to edit and check the Connects cost before I send.</pre>
        </div>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <div class="ap-head">
            <p class="prompt-label">Knowledge Section</p>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>Skills:
Years / background:
Kinds of work I want:
Results I can mention (only real ones):
Testimonials I have permission to use:
I am new to Upwork: yes / no
First-milestone discount is on the table for this job: yes / no
Skip the charity-guarantee line: yes / no</pre>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>If you connect Upwork's official connector, the agent can draft the proposal right in your account. It still won't submit or spend Connects until you confirm.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="kit-section" id="how-to-reply-to-clients-on-upwork">
        <h2>How to reply to clients on Upwork</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>If a prospect agrees to jump on a call, send something along the lines of the following script.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <div class="ap-head">
            <p class="prompt-label">Change the wording so it sounds like you</p>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>Hello [first name],

Thank you for giving me the opportunity to help you.

Let's do a free 30-minute call right here in Upwork Messages. The phone icon in this thread starts a Zoom or voice call. Send me a couple of times that work for you this week.

Pick a time that works for you and we'll take it from there.

Looking forward to talking with you.

[Your name]</pre>
        </div>
        <aside class="note">
          <p class="note-label">Note</p>
          <p>Updated for today: the old version of this script sent a Calendly link. Before a contract, keep scheduling and calls inside Upwork Messages. Once a contract starts, your own Calendly or Zoom is fine.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Next, you should get one of my favorite types of notifications: a client confirming a time to meet.</p>
          <p>All you gotta do is meet with the client and help them understand that you are the person for the job.</p>
          <p>More scripts for scoping, quoting, a polite no, and a follow-up are in the bonus section below.</p>
          <h3>How I'd do this with AI today: replying</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <div class="ap-head">
            <p class="prompt-label">Reply prompt</p>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>Here is the job post: [paste the job post]
Here is the cover letter I sent: [paste it]
Here is the client's message: [paste it]

Draft a short reply that:
1. Thanks them and answers any question they asked.
2. Invites a 30-minute call inside Upwork Messages and asks for a couple of times this week.
3. Asks one or two scoping questions only if their message left something unclear.
Plain, friendly, under 120 words. No em dashes. No outside links or contact info. Sign with my name only: [Your name].</pre>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>With the Upwork connector, the agent can read the thread and draft the reply. It still won't send until you approve it.</p>
        </aside>
